# Boxes

A loom holds more than prose and code. An author sketches a diagram, leaves a note for a reader, and keeps the command that starts a server. An author also meets thoughts too large for the section they start in, and points to a chapter or a discussion of their own. An author writes every one of these as the same block, the box, and a trope can teach Loom a new kind of box without any new syntax.

Loom already reads a box. The chapter "The leaf tokens" gives its shape: a line of three colons with an optional name and braces, then the body, then a line of three colons alone. The chapters "The classifier stage", "The tokeniser stage", and "The document builder" read that shape, and fault a box opened inside another, a run of four or more colons, and a box that never closes. A box that names no kind is a note, and a box of the `Chapter` kind points to a chapter, as the chapter "The multi-file AST" describes. A box's name is its caption, which the editor shows when it folds the box and the woven page shows above a note. This chapter describes the kinds that follow the note and the chapter box.

## Boxes that point to looms

A thought that needs more structure than one box gives it belongs in a loom of its own. A chapter holds sections and a discussion holds topics, each with boxes of their own, and a box points to the loom from the section where the thought began.

A question about how to model a room may need a chapter of its own, and a question nobody has settled may need a discussion. The author then writes a box whose kind names the loom it points to, `{Chapter}` or `{Discussion}`, and whose name is that loom's title.

```
## Room

The room is 1000 steps wide and 700 steps long.

::: Room's 2d system {Chapter}
How to model the room without a game engine: a grid of cells and the things that occupy them.
:::
```

Such a box moves the opposite way to an anchor. An anchor pulls a section's code into the place where the anchor stands. A box that points to a loom pushes a thought out of the text, into a loom of its own, and leaves a short account of the thought behind.

The loom may not exist yet. The box then leaves its loom to be settled: a reference to the box resolves, and the editor marks it as unsettled until the loom is written. When the author opens the loom, the editor writes it with the box's name as its title and the box's body as its opening prose. A new chapter starts with the status `Canvas`, which the chapter "Canvases" describes. A chapter box does all this already, as the chapter "The multi-file AST" describes, and a discussion box does the same once discussions exist.

Once the loom exists, the box's body is the loom's summary. For a chapter, the summary is the opening prose under the chapter's title. For a discussion, it is the opening prose followed by the text of the discussion's conclusions. Each conclusion arrives as plain prose, without its box, because a box never holds another. The loom holds the summary's source, and the box holds a copy.

A copy that differs from its source is a warning, never a silent overwrite, so an edit made outside the editor is never lost. The warning's fix copies the source into the box, and `loom refresh` refreshes every copy at once, the way `loom toc` regenerates the table of contents from the chapters' frontmatter. A chapter box works this way already.

The editor goes further and keeps the two in step as the author types, so an edit to either one reaches the other. A chapter box works this way already, through the language server, as the chapter "The language server" describes. The author can also open the box in a side panel, to edit the loom's opening without leaving the section, in an editor that can show one.

A settled discussion therefore reads in its chapter as a few paragraphs of prose, followed by an arrow that leads to the argument behind them.

A section that holds a box of kind `Chapter` holds no code, and the document builder faults one that does. A discussion box may sit beside code, since a discussion often argues about the code beside it.

## Kinds come from tropes

The kinds of box form an open set, as languages do. The core knows five kinds, because Loom itself gives them their meaning:

- **Note:** a remark for the reader, and the kind of every box that names none.
- **Chapter:** a box that points to a chapter.
- **Discussion:** a box that points to a discussion.
- **Conclusion:** the answer that closes a topic in a discussion, which the chapter "Discussions" describes. A conclusion anywhere else is a fault.
- **Wildcard:** a box that holds the place of code not yet written, with the names the code will provide, which the chapter "Names through the book" describes.

Every other kind comes from a trope, the package the chapter "Tropes" defines. A trope that contributes a kind says how the editor draws a box of that kind, whether the box runs, and what the box's body must satisfy. Two such kinds come first:

- **Mermaid:** a diagram written in Mermaid, which the editor and the woven page draw from the box's body.
- **Run:** a command, which the editor runs on request, showing its output beside the box.

A kind that neither the core nor an installed trope knows is a fault, so a misspelt kind never passes for content.

## Testing boxes

Two test files prove what Loom reads of a box. The first parses one loom at a time and reads back the box marks the parse made and the faults it raised. The second compiles a small corpus, because a chapter box points across looms, to the chapter its name titles.

A loom written inside this loom's code would be read as part of it, so a fixture is a list of its lines, each one quoted, and `lines` joins them into the text a file holds. `anchor` spells an anchor in two pieces for the same reason.

`parse` reads a loom alone, through the layer the compiler parses with. `wefts` gathers every line of a parsed loom: the document's preamble, then each section's preamble and code. `boxes` keeps the box marks among those lines. `diagnosticsIn` walks the whole tree and gathers the diagnostics each node's health carries, so a case sees every fault the parse raised, wherever it sits. `woven` builds a single loom into a corpus and weaves it, for the case that reads a box on the woven page.

=>

import { describe, expect, it } from 'bun:test'
import { Array, Effect, Layer, Option } from 'effect'
import type { Diagnostic } from '@athrio/loom-ast/LoomNode'
import type { LoomDocument } from '@athrio/loom-ast/LoomAst'
import { boxKind, type BoxToken } from '@athrio/loom-ast/LoomTokens'
import { boxOf, type LoomWeft } from '@athrio/loom-ast/Weft'
import { LoomCorpusAstBuilder } from '#ast/LoomCorpusAstBuilder'
import { WeaveBuilder } from '../src/weave/WeaveBuilder'
import { parseDocument, ParseLayer } from './parse'

const lines = (...rows: ReadonlyArray<string>): string => `${rows.join('\n')}\n`

const anchor = (name: string): string => `::${'['}${name}]`

const parse = (src: string): LoomDocument =>
  Effect.runSync(parseDocument(src).pipe(Effect.provide(ParseLayer)))

const wefts = (doc: LoomDocument): ReadonlyArray<LoomWeft> => [
  ...doc.preamble,
  ...Array.flatMap(doc.sections, (section) => [...section.preamble, ...section.code]),
]

const boxes = (doc: LoomDocument): ReadonlyArray<BoxToken> =>
  Array.getSomes(Array.map(wefts(doc), boxOf))

const diagnosticsIn = (node: unknown): ReadonlyArray<Diagnostic> => {
  if (node === null || typeof node !== 'object') return []
  if (globalThis.Array.isArray(node)) return node.flatMap(diagnosticsIn)
  const own =
    'health' in node
      ? (node as { health: { diagnostics: ReadonlyArray<Diagnostic> } }).health.diagnostics
      : []
  return [
    ...own,
    ...Object.entries(node).flatMap(([key, value]) =>
      key === 'health' ? [] : diagnosticsIn(value),
    ),
  ]
}

const woven = (path: string, text: string) =>
  Effect.runSync(
    Effect.gen(function* () {
      const builder = yield* LoomCorpusAstBuilder
      const module = yield* builder.build(
        { read: () => Effect.succeed(text), list: Option.none() },
        path,
      )
      const weaver = yield* WeaveBuilder
      return yield* weaver.build({ modules: new Map([[path, module]]) })
    }).pipe(Effect.provide(Layer.mergeAll(LoomCorpusAstBuilder.layer, WeaveBuilder.layer))),
  )

## A note between two lines of colons

A line of three colons alone opens a note, and the next such line closes it. The note's body is inert: a heading inside it opens no section, and an arrow inside it opens no code. A box that names no kind is a note, and this one has no name either. It raises no fault, and the parse gives its source back byte for byte. A note may also stand before a loom's first heading, in the document's preamble.

=>

describe('a note', () => {
  const note = lines(
    '# Chapter',
    '',
    'Before the note.',
    '',
    ':::',
    '# Not a heading',
    '=>',
    'Not code either.',
    ':::',
    '',
    'After the note.',
  )

  it('opens with three colons alone and closes with three more', () => {
    expect(boxes(parse(note)).map((box) => box.side)).toEqual(['open', 'close'])
  })

  it('keeps its body inert, so a heading or an arrow inside it opens nothing', () => {
    const doc = parse(note)
    expect(doc.sections).toHaveLength(1)
    expect(doc.sections[0]!.code.some((w) => w.type === 'ArrowWeft')).toBe(false)
  })

  it('is a note, with no name', () => {
    const [open] = boxes(parse(note))
    expect(boxKind(open!)).toBe('Note')
    expect(open!.name).toBeUndefined()
  })

  it('raises no fault, and reconstructs the source verbatim', () => {
    const doc = parse(note)
    expect(diagnosticsIn(doc)).toEqual([])
    expect(doc.source).toBe(note)
  })

  it('opens in the document preamble too', () => {
    const doc = parse(lines(':::', 'A remark before any heading.', ':::', '', '# Chapter'))
    expect(boxes(doc).map((box) => box.side)).toEqual(['open', 'close'])
  })
})

## What an opening line says

An opening line may carry a name and braces after its colons. A label in the braces names the box's kind, and so does a `kind` parameter, whatever the case of its letters. Braces count only at the end of the line, so braces in the middle of a name stay part of the name. A box may also carry braces and no name. The token of the name covers the name's text exactly, so the editor can underline it.

=>

describe('an opening line', () => {
  const opener = (line: string): BoxToken =>
    boxes(parse(lines('# Chapter', '', line, 'Body.', ':::')))[0]!

  it('reads its name and its kind from a label', () => {
    const box = opener('::: Why a grid {Note}')
    expect(box.name?.value).toBe('Why a grid')
    expect(box.specifier?.type).toBe('Specifier')
    expect(boxKind(box)).toBe('Note')
  })

  it('reads its kind from a parameter, in any case of letters', () => {
    const box = opener('::: Why a grid {kind=note}')
    expect(box.specifier?.type).toBe('Parameters')
    expect(boxKind(box)).toBe('Note')
  })

  it('keeps braces that do not end the line in its name', () => {
    const box = opener('::: Why {x} matters')
    expect(box.name?.value).toBe('Why {x} matters')
    expect(box.specifier).toBeUndefined()
  })

  it('opens a box with braces and no name', () => {
    const box = opener('::: {Note}')
    expect(box.name).toBeUndefined()
    expect(boxKind(box)).toBe('Note')
  })

  it('places its name on the text it names', () => {
    const src = lines('# Chapter', '', '::: Why a grid {Note}', 'Body.', ':::')
    const box = boxes(parse(src))[0]!
    const name = Option.getOrThrow(Option.fromNullishOr(box.name))
    expect(src.slice(name.position.start.offset, name.position.end.offset)).toBe('Why a grid')
  })
})

## The keys and kinds a box takes

A box writes its braces as an anchor does, but the two take different words in them. The core gives a box three kinds, `Note`, `Chapter`, and `Conclusion`, and one key, `kind`, so any other kind or key on a box is a fault. An anchor's braces answer to the anchor's own list, which holds the kinds `To be settled` and `Reference`.

=>

describe('the braces on a box', () => {
  const faults = (line: string): ReadonlyArray<string> =>
    diagnosticsIn(parse(lines('# Chapter', '', line, 'Body.', ':::'))).map((d) => d.message)

  it('fault a kind the core does not give a box', () => {
    expect(faults('::: Cells {Mermaid}')).toEqual([
      'Unknown kind: a box takes no kind `Mermaid`. It takes `Note`, `Chapter`, and `Conclusion`.',
    ])
  })

  it('fault a key a box does not take', () => {
    expect(faults('::: Cells {kind=Note width=480}')).toEqual([
      'Unknown key: a box takes no `width` key. It takes `kind`.',
    ])
  })

  it('leave an anchor its own keys and kinds', () => {
    const found = diagnosticsIn(
      parse(
        lines(
          '# Chapter',
          '',
          '=>',
          '',
          `${anchor('Greeting')}{Mermaid}`,
          '',
          '## Greeting',
          '',
          '=>',
          '',
          'x',
        ),
      ),
    ).map((d) => d.message)
    expect(found).toContain(
      'Unknown kind: an anchor takes no kind `Mermaid`. It takes `To be settled` and `Reference`.',
    )
  })
})

## Colons out of place

Three colons inside an open box would open a second box inside the first. A box never holds another, so the line is a stray, and a fault. A run of four or more colons opens and closes nothing, and its fault carries a fix that writes three.

A box that nothing closes runs to the end of its loom. The headings after it open no sections, and the box's opening line carries the fault.

=>

describe('a stray line of colons', () => {
  it('faults three colons that would open a box inside another', () => {
    const doc = parse(lines('# Chapter', '', '::: Outer', '::: Inner', ':::', '', 'After.'))
    expect(boxes(doc).map((box) => box.side)).toEqual(['open', 'stray', 'close'])
    expect(diagnosticsIn(doc).map((d) => d.message)).toEqual([
      'Box inside a box: a box never holds another, so this line opens nothing. Close the open box with `:::` on a line of its own, then open this one.',
    ])
  })

  it('faults a run of four or more colons, with a fix that writes three', () => {
    const doc = parse(lines('# Chapter', '', '::::', 'Prose.'))
    expect(boxes(doc).map((box) => box.side)).toEqual(['stray'])
    const [fault] = diagnosticsIn(doc)
    expect(fault!.message).toBe(
      'Too many colons: a box line starts with three colons, so `::::` opens and closes nothing. Write `:::`.',
    )
    expect(fault!.fixes).toEqual([{ title: 'Write `:::`', text: ':::' }])
  })
})

describe('a box that never closes', () => {
  it('faults its opening line, and swallows the headings after it', () => {
    const doc = parse(lines('# Chapter', '', '::: Open', 'Body.', '', '# Swallowed'))
    expect(doc.sections).toHaveLength(1)
    expect(diagnosticsIn(doc).map((d) => d.message)).toEqual([
      'Unclosed box: this box runs to the end of the loom, because nothing closes it. Close it with `:::` on a line of its own.',
    ])
  })
})

## Colons that are no box line

Colons mark a box only in prose. Inside a fence quoted in prose they are quoted text, and after an arrow they are code.

A box's body opens nothing, not even a quote. So a fence inside a box quotes nothing. The next line of three colons still closes the box, and the anchors in the box and after it stay live.

=>

describe('colons that are no box line', () => {
  it('stay quoted inside a fence in prose', () => {
    const doc = parse(lines('# Chapter', '', '```', '::: Quoted {Note}', ':::', '```'))
    expect(boxes(doc)).toEqual([])
  })

  it('stay code after an arrow', () => {
    const doc = parse(lines('# Chapter', '', '=>', '', ':::', 'x'))
    expect(boxes(doc)).toEqual([])
  })

  it('open no quote inside a box', () => {
    const doc = parse(lines('# Chapter', '', ':::', '```', ':::', '', '# Next'))
    expect(boxes(doc).map((box) => box.side)).toEqual(['open', 'close'])
    expect(doc.sections).toHaveLength(2)
  })

  it('leave the anchors after a fence inside a box live', () => {
    const doc = parse(
      lines(
        '# Chapter',
        '',
        ':::',
        '```',
        `A link to ${anchor('Next')}.`,
        ':::',
        '',
        `See ${anchor('Next')}.`,
        '',
        '# Next',
      ),
    )
    const names = Array.flatMap(wefts(doc), (weft) =>
      'anchors' in weft ? weft.anchors.map((found) => found.name.value) : [],
    )
    expect(names).toEqual(['Next', 'Next'])
  })
})

## A box on the woven page

The weave splits a note out of the prose into a block of its own. A box of any other kind stays in the prose around it, colons and all, as the author wrote it.

=>

describe('a box on the woven page', () => {
  it('leaves a box of another kind in the prose around it', () => {
    const boxed = lines(
      '---',
      'Part I, Chapter 1: A greeting',
      'Language: Prose',
      '---',
      '',
      '# A greeting',
      '',
      'Before.',
      '::: Cells {Mermaid}',
      'flowchart LR',
      ':::',
      'After.',
    )
    const page = woven('/corpus/boxed.loom', boxed).pages[0]!
    expect(page.blocks.filter((b) => b.type === 'note')).toEqual([])
    const prose = page.blocks.flatMap((b) => (b.type === 'prose' ? [b.markdown] : []))
    expect(prose).toEqual(['Before.\n::: Cells {Mermaid}\nflowchart LR\n:::\nAfter.'])
  })
})

